---
import assets from '../data/assets.json';
import Figure from './Figure.astro';

const asset = (id:string) => (assets as any)[id];
---

<article class="case-study validation-case">
  <header class="case-hero validation-hero">
    <p class="case-eyebrow">ISO 18571 model validation</p>
    <h1>Quantitative FE model validation</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">Establish model credibility before using simulation to compare restraint designs.</p>
    <p class="case-role">I built and calibrated both LS-DYNA benchmark environments, matched occupant posture and restraint setup, processed the comparison channels, calculated ISO 18571 ratings, and used the quantitative and kinematic evidence to decide which responses could support the anchor-position study.</p>

    <div class="case-metrics validation-metrics" aria-label="Validation scope">
      <div><strong class="validation-metric-value">0.71</strong><span class="validation-metric-label">Average ISO 18571</span><p>in each benchmark</p></div>
      <div><strong class="validation-metric-value">2</strong><span class="validation-metric-label">Physical benchmark configurations</span></div>
      <div><strong class="validation-metric-value">10</strong><span class="validation-metric-label">Quantitative signals</span><p>evaluated per benchmark</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="validation-question-note">Validation combined waveform agreement with occupant kinematics; a plausible animation alone was not treated as sufficient evidence.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="case-section" id="physical-benchmarks">
    <h2>Physical benchmarks</h2>
    <div class="bench-grid compact-bench-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>Rigid Bench</h3>
        <dl class="bench-details">
          <div><dt>Pulse</dt><dd>23 g frontal</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd>3 matched TC/PMG sled tests</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Restraint</dt><dd>3-point belt</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Semi-Rigid Bench</h3>
        <dl class="bench-details">
          <div><dt>Pulse</dt><dd>9 g</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd>2 published sled tests</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Restraint</dt><dd>4-point, 2-belt benchmark system</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="positioning-block featured-technical-figure">
      <h3>Positioning the bench models</h3>
      <Figure asset={asset('G01')} number={1} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" id="quantitative-signals">
    <h2>Signals evaluated</h2>
    <div class="signal-list signal-list-compact">
      <span>Head resultant acceleration</span>
      <span>Four IR-TRACC chest deflections</span>
      <span>Pelvis resultant acceleration</span>
      <span>Left and right iliac forces</span>
      <span>Shoulder belt load</span>
      <span>Lap belt load</span>
    </div>
    <p class="limitation-note">Posture, forward excursion, torso rotation, pitch, and belt migration were reviewed separately through synchronized kinematic images; torso rotation was not one of the ten ISO-scored signals.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" id="method">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <div class="validation-phase-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Build and position</h3>
        <p>Reproduce bench geometry, pulse, restraints, initial posture, belt routing, and available initial measurements.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Compare and score</h3>
        <p>Apply SAE J211 filtering, compare timing, magnitude and shape, and calculate ISO 18571 for corresponding signals.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Interpret</h3>
        <p>Use kinematics and load paths to explain local discrepancies rather than relying on a single average score.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" id="quantitative-result">
    <h2>Quantitative results</h2>
    <div class="validation-result-grid validation-result-compact">
      <article>
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Rigid Bench</p>
        <strong>0.71 <span>average ISO 18571</span></strong>
        <p><b>Strongest agreement:</b> pelvis acceleration and shoulder/lap belt loads.</p>
        <p><b>Main limitation:</b> left-lower chest response, torso rotation, and belt migration.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Semi-Rigid Bench</p>
        <strong>0.71 <span>average ISO 18571</span></strong>
        <p><b>Strongest agreement:</b> head and pelvis acceleration and shoulder/lap belt loads.</p>
        <p><b>Main limitation:</b> iliac-force response and simplified restraint-system compliance.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="validation-summary-figure technical-plate">
      <Figure asset={asset('A02')} number={2} />
    </div>

    <div class="absolute-differences">
      <h3>Where the model differed</h3>
      <div class="difference-list compact-difference-list">
        <article><h4>Rigid Bench iliac force</h4><p>Peak response approximately 1 kN lower than the physical tests.</p></article>
        <article><h4>Semi-Rigid belt load</h4><p>Before compliance calibration: approximately 10 ms earlier and 0.5 kN (15%) lower.</p></article>
        <article><h4>Semi-Rigid pelvis acceleration</h4><p>Before compliance calibration: 10 ms (13%) earlier and more than 5 g (24%) lower.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="limitation-note">These differences were retained as validation limitations rather than hidden by the overall 0.71 averages.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" id="engineering-interpretation">
    <h2>Engineering interpretation</h2>
    <div class="decision-list validation-interpretation">
      <article><h3>Rigid Bench</h3><p>The model matched early forward motion and restraint timing, but the physical ATD developed greater torso rotation and more shoulder-belt migration.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Semi-Rigid Bench</h3><p>The primary loading phase and forward motion compared well after belt-system compliance was calibrated; smaller late differences remained in pitch and arm extension.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Chest instrumentation</h3><p>The physical right-lower chest sensor bottomed out in both benchmark datasets, producing a plateau the FE model did not reproduce.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Iliac force</h3><p>Local iliac-force disagreement remained a sensor/load-path limitation even where lap-belt load and pelvis acceleration agreed well.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section validation-decision" id="decision">
    <h2>Decision</h2>
    <p>The models were sufficiently representative for a <strong>comparative anchor-position sensitivity study</strong>, particularly for chest deflection, pelvis acceleration, belt loading, and overall occupant motion. The 0.71 averages were not treated as blanket validation of every local signal.</p>
    <p class="sharing-boundary"><strong>Public-sharing boundary:</strong> This summary uses methods and results disclosed in the thesis. Raw test files, proprietary model inputs, and restricted partner material are excluded.</p>
  </section>
</article>
